        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "6b12a06a-1d83-5f45-bcf9-897a0fec7bd2",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Lincoln Steffens, Monique Wittig, Doc Ames\n- Works: McClure's, \"Tweed Days in St. Louis\", \"The Shame of the Cities\"\n- Topics: feminism, gender roles, organized crime in Minneapolis\n\nFacts:\n- Lincoln Steffens was born on April 6, 1866, and he was a New York reporter.\n- In 1903, organized crime in Minneapolis attracted national attention when mayor Doc Ames was exposed by Lincoln Steffens in the book \"The Shame of the Cities\".\n- Lincoln Steffens launched a series of articles in McClure's called \"Tweed Days in St. Louis\", and those articles were later published together as the book \"The Shame of the Cities\".\n- Monique Wittig was born on July 13, 1935, and she was a French author and feminist theorist.\n- Monique Wittig wrote about overcoming socially enforced gender roles and coined the phrase \"heterosexual contract\".\n- Lincoln Steffens died on August 9, 1936.\n- Monique Wittig died on January 3, 2003.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
